zimr is overwhelmingly a pure-Zig port of raylib 6.0 by Ramon Santamaria
(@raysan5).  The license above is the same zlib/libpng license raylib
itself uses, in line with the spirit of clauses 1-3.  Files derived from
raylib are individually marked as such in their headers.

zimr also incorporates code adapted from other open-source projects
under their own licenses.



# Third-party licenses and attribution

zimr is licensed under the zlib/libpng license (see `LICENSE`).
This file documents the external projects whose code, designs, or
data have been adapted into zimr's source tree, with each
upstream's license, author(s), and the specific zimr files derived
from each upstream.

When the upstream license requires that the original notice be
preserved in source-form distributions, that notice is reproduced
verbatim below.  zimr aims to be over-attributive rather than
under-attributive — if you contributed to any of the upstreams
listed and your name should appear here, please open an issue or
PR.

---

## raylib — Ramon Santamaria (`@raysan5`) and contributors

- **Upstream:** https://github.com/raysan5/raylib
- **License:** zlib/libpng
- **Author:** Ramon Santamaria and the raylib contributors

zimr is broadly a pure-Zig port of raylib 6.0 for the
wasm32-wasi + WebGL2 target.  raylib is the upstream from which
the bulk of zimr's API, data layouts, and algorithms derive.  Our
zlib/libpng license was chosen specifically to honor raylib's
license, so the same restrictions you've signed up for under
raylib carry through to anyone using zimr.

The following zimr files are direct ports (in whole or in part)
of named raylib source files:

| zimr file              | raylib source                              |
| ---------------------- | ------------------------------------------ |
| `src/rlgl.zig`         | `rlgl.h` (CPU + GPU paths, WebGL 2 only)   |
| `src/rlsw.zig`         | `rlsw.h` (experimental software renderer)  |
| `src/rlsw_pixel.zig`   | `rlsw.h` (pixel-format codecs subset)      |
| `src/drawing.zig`      | `rshapes.c`, `rtextures.c`, `rtext.c`, `rmodels.c`, rlgl shader-management |
| `src/runtime.zig`      | `rcore.c` (core + input subsets, no GLFW)  |
| `src/codecs.zig`       | `rtextures.c` (PNG decoder); `rtext.c` (TTF + glyph baking); gltf, rectpack |
| `src/sound.zig`        | `raudio.c` (Wave/Sound/Music, Web Audio backend) |
| `src/types.zig`        | data type definitions + the named-color palette |
| `src/enums.zig`        | enum definitions                       |

Files that are zimr-original (not raylib-derived) and so are NOT
in the table above:
`src/zimr.zig` (App / Frame runtime), `src/loader.zig`,
`src/clock.zig`, `src/rng.zig`, `src/logger.zig`,
`src/allocator.zig` (the freeMany helper), `src/png.zig` (we wrote
this from scratch — pure-Zig, no stb_image lineage),
`src/leak_test.zig`, `src/multiapp_test.zig`,
`src/web/{dom,gl,fetch,audio}.zig`, `examples/*.zig`,
`tests/smoke.ts`, `build.zig`.

raylib's full notice is reproduced here for clauses-2-and-3
compliance:

    Copyright (c) 2013-2024 Ramon Santamaria (@raysan5)

    This software is provided "as-is", without any express or implied warranty.
    In no event will the authors be held liable for any damages arising from
    the use of this software.

    Permission is granted to anyone to use this software for any purpose,
    including commercial applications, and to alter it and redistribute it
    freely, subject to the following restrictions:

      1. The origin of this software must not be misrepresented; you must not
         claim that you wrote the original software. If you use this software
         in a product, an acknowledgment in the product documentation would be
         appreciated but is not required.

      2. Altered source versions must be plainly marked as such, and must not
         be misrepresented as being the original software.

      3. This notice may not be removed or altered from any source distribution.

